From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: grf53 <grf0503@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 14:22:19 +0900
Subject: macos-krunfw-path: open libkrunfw beside the executable on macOS, as on Linux and Windows

## Why virtx needs it

The guest kernel sits beside the binary that opens it, on every host.
dyld looks for a dlopen name with no slash only in DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH and the fallback path, never near the caller, so on macOS whoever ran the server had to set an environment variable.
KRUNFW_NAME is @loader_path/libkrunfw.5.dylib there, which dyld resolves against the directory of what did the dlopen.

## What it changes

One string, so that macOS obeys the same rule as the other two: **the guest kernel sits
beside the binary that opens it.**

`cargo xtask build server-*` and `cargo xtask test` put it there, and libkrun opens it
by name at run time rather than linking it. On Windows a bare name is enough, because the loader searches the
executable's own directory before anywhere else. On Linux it is enough too, because
`dlopen` consults the *calling object's* `DT_RUNPATH` and the link sets that to `$ORIGIN`
— see `.cargo/config.toml`.

dyld does neither. A `dlopen` argument with no slash in it is looked for in
`DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` and then `DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH`
(`$HOME/lib:/usr/local/lib:/usr/lib`), and nowhere near the caller. `@rpath` and
`@loader_path` do not help: they are prefixes dyld expands inside a *path*, so a name
that is not one is never resolved against them, whatever `LC_RPATH` the binary carries.
So `KRUNFW_NAME` is `@loader_path/libkrunfw.5.dylib` there — a path, which dyld then
resolves against the directory of whatever did the `dlopen`.

What this replaces is an environment variable that whoever runs the binary had to set,
which a wrapper can do for `cargo xtask run` and nothing can do for an installed binary
someone runs by hand.

## When it can go

When upstream opens libkrunfw by a path relative to its loader on macOS, or takes the path from the embedder.
---
 src/libkrun/src/api/payload.rs | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libkrun/src/api/payload.rs b/src/libkrun/src/api/payload.rs
index 00c61c9e..b106abdc 100644
--- a/src/libkrun/src/api/payload.rs
+++ b/src/libkrun/src/api/payload.rs
@@ -273,8 +273,18 @@ const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "libkrunfw.so.5";
 const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "libkrunfw-sev.so.5";
 #[cfg(all(target_os = "linux", feature = "tdx"))]
 const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "libkrunfw-tdx.so.5";
+// `@loader_path/` and not a leaf name, which is what every other target here uses. The
+// kernel is shipped beside the binary that opens it -- see `virtx-uvm/build.rs` --
+// and on the other two platforms a leaf name finds it there: Windows searches the
+// executable's own directory first, and Linux the calling object's `DT_RUNPATH`, which
+// the link sets to `$ORIGIN`. dyld does neither. A `dlopen` argument with no slash in it
+// is looked for in `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` and then `DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH`, and nowhere
+// near the caller -- `@rpath` and `@loader_path` are prefixes dyld expands in a *path*,
+// so a name that is not one is never resolved against them. Spelling the prefix is what
+// puts macOS under the same rule as the others rather than under an environment variable
+// that has to be set by whoever runs the binary.
 #[cfg(target_os = "macos")]
-const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "libkrunfw.5.dylib";
+const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "@loader_path/libkrunfw.5.dylib";
 #[cfg(target_os = "windows")]
 const KRUNFW_NAME: &str = "libkrunfw.dll";
 
